Exactly — stotting.

That’s the gazelle/antelope behavior you were reaching for: the high, stiff-legged leaps in front of a predator. One major interpretation is pursuit deterrence: a conspicuous, potentially costly display that advertises condition and says, in effect, “I’m a bad target; chasing me will cost you.”

So your original distinction was right:

bluff = signal exceeds actual backing
stotting = signal is backed by capability, and its credibility partly comes from demonstrating surplus capability

The transferable Pantheon handle is broader than the literal locomotion: Stotting = deliberately exposing or spending a measurable reserve to make hidden capability legible and alter another agent’s risk/reward calculation.

That can cross species, media, and domains because the receiver does not need to understand the signaler’s subjective meaning; it only needs to translate the display into something consequential to itself:

\text{display}
\rightarrow
\text{inferred reserve/capability}
\rightarrow
\text{expected cost of engagement}
\rightarrow
\text{behavior change}

And there’s a particularly elegant form you already caught: “I can afford to spend some of the resource I would need to beat you, merely to show you that I have enough of it.”

That is a very strong signal.